# Words 401-450 (adjectives ADJ-001..050). Action Sequence is None for non-verbs.
R = [
("ADJ-001","difficult – more difficult – most difficult","'A' needs a noun after it. With only an adjective, drop 'a': 'This is difficult.'","DIF-FI-CULT: three hard syllables for a hard word.","'Difficult' is more formal than 'hard'. 'A difficult person' = someone hard to deal with.","Write a sentence about the most difficult subject for you.",None,"difficult vs hard","Same meaning for tasks. Hard is more common in speech; hard also means firm to touch."),
("ADJ-002","comfortable – more comfortable – most comfortable","'Comfortable' is followed by -ing or 'with', not 'to + verb'.","COMFORT-ABLE: ABLE to give comfort. Say it 'KUMF-tuh-bul' (3 syllables).","'Make yourself comfortable' = relax, feel at home.","Write a sentence about a place where you feel comfortable.",None,"comfortable vs comfort","Comfortable = adjective (a comfortable chair). Comfort = noun or verb (the comfort of home)."),
("ADJ-003","dangerous – more dangerous – most dangerous","Use 'dangerous to + verb' (+ preposition if needed): 'dangerous to swim in'.","DANGER-OUS: full of DANGER.","'Dangerous' warns people: 'It's dangerous to cross here.'","Write a sentence about a dangerous road near your home.",None,"dangerous vs risky","Dangerous = likely to cause harm. Risky = has a chance of loss or failure (a risky plan)."),
("ADJ-004","popular – more popular – most popular","Use 'popular WITH' people, not 'by'.","POP-ULAR: POPS up everywhere because everyone likes it.","'Popular' also means for ordinary people: 'popular culture'.","Write a sentence about a place that is popular with tourists.",None,"popular vs famous","Famous = known by many people. Popular = liked by many people."),
("ADJ-005","interesting – more interesting – most interesting","-ED describes how a person feels (interested). -ING describes what causes it (interesting).","-ING gives the feeling; -ED gets the feeling. The book is interesting; I am interested.","'That's interesting' can also politely mean 'I'm not sure I agree'.","Write two sentences: one with 'interested' and one with 'interesting'.",None,"interesting vs interested","Interesting = causes interest (an interesting film). Interested = feels interest (I'm interested in films)."),
("ADJ-006","expensive – more expensive – most expensive","'Very much' doesn't go before an adjective; use 'very' or 'much more'.","EX-PENSIVE: money goes OUT ('ex') of your purse.","'Pricey' and 'costly' are other ways to say expensive.","Write a sentence about the most expensive thing you own.",None,"expensive vs costly","Expensive is everyday. Costly also means causing loss: 'a costly mistake'."),
("ADJ-007","important – more important – most important","After 'It is important that…', use the base verb (you come), not 'should'.","IM-PORT-ANT: things that are IMPORTed matter to the country.","'The most important thing is…' is a clear way to give a main point.","Write a sentence about the most important thing in your life.",None,"important vs necessary","Important = has great value. Necessary = must be done or needed."),
("ADJ-008","confident – more confident – most confident","Use 'confident ABOUT' or 'confident OF', not 'for'.","CON-FIDENT: FIDE = faith (like 'fidelity'): faith in yourself.","'Confident' is a positive quality in interviews.","Write a sentence about something you feel confident about.",None,"confident vs confidential","Confident = sure of yourself. Confidential = secret (confidential documents)."),
("ADJ-009","frustrated – more frustrated – most frustrated","The thing causing the feeling is 'frustrating'; the person feels 'frustrated'.","-ED = the person feels it. -ING = the thing causes it.","Common complaint: 'This is so frustrating!'","Write a sentence about something that makes you frustrated.",None,"frustrated vs frustrating","Frustrated = how you feel. Frustrating = what makes you feel it."),
("ADJ-010","grateful – more grateful – most grateful","Use 'grateful FOR' a thing and 'grateful TO' a person.","GRATE-FUL: full of GRATitude.","'I'd be grateful if you could…' is a polite request in emails.","Write a sentence about something you are grateful for.",None,"grateful vs thankful","Same meaning. Grateful is slightly more formal."),
("ADJ-011","anxious – more anxious – most anxious","Use 'anxious ABOUT' something that worries you. 'Anxious for' means eager for.","ANXIOUS has 'X' — like crossed fingers when you're worried.","'Anxious to' can also mean eager: 'She's anxious to start.'","Write a sentence about something that makes you anxious.",None,"anxious vs nervous","Nervous = worried before a specific event. Anxious = worried, often for longer or about the unknown."),
("ADJ-012","embarrassed – more embarrassed – most embarrassed","The situation is 'embarrassing'; the person feels 'embarrassed'.","EMBARRASSED: double R, double S — a doubly red face.","Very commonly misspelt: e-m-b-a-r-r-a-s-s-e-d.","Write a sentence about an embarrassing moment.",None,"embarrassed vs ashamed","Embarrassed = awkward in front of others. Ashamed = feel guilty about something wrong you did."),
("ADJ-013","nervous – more nervous – most nervous","Use 'nervous ABOUT' something, not 'of'.","NERV-OUS: your NERVES are shaking.","'Nervous' is normal before exams and interviews.","Write a sentence about when you feel nervous.",None,"nervous vs scared","Nervous = worried and uneasy. Scared = afraid of something that might hurt you."),
("ADJ-014","proud – prouder – proudest","Use 'proud OF' something or someone, not 'for'.","PROUD: your chest goes out, like the round letters O and U.","'I'm proud of you' is a powerful thing to say to a child or friend.","Write a sentence about something you are proud of.",None,"proud vs arrogant","Proud = pleased about an achievement (good). Arrogant = thinks they're better than others (bad)."),
("ADJ-015","tall – taller – tallest","One-syllable adjectives take -er, not 'more': taller.","TALL: two tall Ls standing side by side.","'Tall' is for people, buildings and trees. 'High' is for mountains and walls.","Write a sentence comparing your height with a family member's.",None,"tall vs high","Tall = height of people, trees, buildings. High = distance above the ground (a high shelf, a high mountain)."),
("ADJ-016","short – shorter – shortest","One-syllable adjectives take -er, not 'more': shorter.","SHORT: the opposite of TALL and of LONG.","'Short of money' = not having enough money.","Write a sentence about a short trip you took.",None,"short vs brief","Short = little height or length. Brief = short in time (a brief meeting)."),
("ADJ-017","thin – thinner – thinnest","One-syllable adjectives take -er, and 'thin' doubles the n: thinner.","THIN → THINNER: double N.","'Thin' for people can sound negative; 'slim' is more polite.","Write a sentence about a book or object that is very thin.",None,"thin vs slim","Thin = little flesh or width (can sound unhealthy). Slim = thin in an attractive way."),
("ADJ-018","fat – fatter – fattest","One-syllable adjectives take -er, and 'fat' doubles the t: fatter.","FAT → FATTER: double T.","Calling a person 'fat' is rude. Softer words: 'heavy', 'overweight'.","Write a sentence using 'fat' for food, not people (e.g. low-fat milk).",None,"fat vs overweight","Fat is direct and can hurt. Overweight is a neutral, medical-sounding word."),
("ADJ-019","slim – slimmer – slimmest","One-syllable adjectives take -er, and 'slim' doubles the m: slimmer.","SLIM → SLIMMER: double M.","'A slim chance' = a very small chance.","Write a sentence using 'a slim chance'.",None,"slim vs thin","Slim = attractively thin. Thin = little flesh or width, sometimes too little."),
("ADJ-020","chubby – chubbier – chubbiest","Adjectives ending in -y take -ier: chubbier, not 'more chubby'.","CHUBBY: the round sound, like a baby's cheeks.","Usually affectionate: 'chubby cheeks'.","Write a sentence about a chubby baby or pet.",None,"chubby vs fat","Chubby = a little fat in a cute way. Fat is more direct and can be unkind."),
("ADJ-021","heavy – heavier – heaviest","Adjectives ending in -y take -ier: heavier.","HEAVY: the -Y becomes -IER, like HAPPY → HAPPIER.","'Heavy rain', 'heavy traffic' = a lot of it.","Write a sentence about heavy rain or traffic in your city.",None,"heavy vs hard","Heavy = weighs a lot. Hard = firm, or difficult."),
("ADJ-022","light – lighter – lightest","One-syllable adjectives take -er: lighter.","LIGHT as a feather, or LIGHT like a bulb — one word, two meanings.","'Light' also means pale: 'light blue'.","Write a sentence about something light you carry every day.",None,"light vs lite","Light is the correct spelling. 'Lite' is only used in product names (Coke Lite)."),
("ADJ-023","bright – brighter – brightest","One-syllable adjectives take -er: brighter.","BRIGHT: B for Bulb.","'A bright student' = intelligent.","Write a sentence about a bright colour you like.",None,"bright vs light","Bright = full of strong light or colour. Light = not dark, or pale."),
("ADJ-024","dark – darker – darkest","One-syllable adjectives take -er: darker.","DARK: the opposite of LIGHT.","'Dark' is also used for skin tone; be careful and respectful.","Write a sentence about a dark place that scares you.",None,"dark vs black","Black is a colour. Dark = having little light, or a deep shade of a colour (dark blue)."),
("ADJ-025","shiny – shinier – shiniest","After 'very', use the adjective 'shiny', not the verb 'shine'.","SHINE → SHINY: drop the E, add Y.","'Shiny new car' is a common phrase.","Write a sentence about something shiny in your home.",None,"shiny vs bright","Shiny = reflects light (metal, glass). Bright = gives out light or has strong colour."),
("ADJ-026","dull – duller – dullest","'Bored' describes how people feel. For the thing itself, use 'dull' or 'boring'.","DULL: a dull knife, a dull day — nothing sharp or exciting.","'Dull' also means not bright: 'dull colours'.","Write a sentence about a dull day at work or school.",None,"dull vs boring","Boring = not interesting. Dull = boring, or not bright, or not sharp.","The lecture was bored.","The lecture was dull."),
("ADJ-027","smooth – smoother – smoothest","After 'was' (a linking verb), use the adjective 'smooth', not the adverb.","SMOOTH: say it slowly, it sounds smooth.","'Smooth' also means without problems: 'a smooth journey'.","Write a sentence about something with a smooth surface.",None,"smooth vs soft","Smooth = even, no bumps. Soft = easy to press, not hard."),
("ADJ-028","rough – rougher – roughest","Before a noun, use the adjective 'rough', not the adverb 'roughly'.","ROUGH has 'GH' pronounced 'f', like TOUGH and ENOUGH.","'A rough idea' = an approximate idea. 'Rough day' = a hard day.","Write a sentence giving a rough idea of your monthly expenses.",None,"rough vs roughly","Rough = adjective (a rough estimate). Roughly = adverb meaning 'about' (roughly ₹500)."),
("ADJ-029","sharp – sharper – sharpest","After 'is', use the adjective 'sharp', not the adverb.","SHARP: the letters end in a point, like a knife.","'At 9 sharp' = exactly at 9.","Write a sentence using 'sharp' about time or intelligence.",None,"sharp vs pointed","Sharp = can cut, or quick-thinking. Pointed = has a thin end (a pointed stick)."),
("ADJ-030","blunt – blunter – bluntest","A singular countable noun needs 'a': 'a blunt person'.","BLUNT: the word ends flat, like a blunt knife.","For people, blunt = very direct. It can seem rude.","Write a sentence about someone who is blunt but honest.",None,"blunt vs rude","Blunt = very direct and honest. Rude = impolite. Blunt people aren't always trying to be rude.","He is blunt person.","He is a blunt person."),
("ADJ-031","round – rounder – roundest","'Round' already describes the shape. Say 'round' or 'round in shape', not 'round shape'.","ROUND: the O in the word is round.","'Round' also means a stage in a game: 'the first round'.","Write a sentence about something round on your table.",None,"round vs circular","Same meaning. Circular is more formal or technical.","The earth is round shape.","The earth is round."),
("ADJ-032","square – (not usually compared)","'Square' already means the shape, so 'shape' is extra.","SQUARE: four equal sides, like a chessboard box.","'Square feet' measures area: 'a 1,000 sq ft flat'.","Write a sentence about something square in your room.",None,"square vs rectangular","Square = four equal sides. Rectangular = two long and two short sides.","Draw a square shape box.","Draw a square box."),
("ADJ-033","straight – straighter – straightest","'Straight' is both adjective and adverb. 'Straightly' isn't used.","STRAIGHT has 'GH' silent: say 'strayt'.","Directions: 'Go straight and turn left.'","Write a sentence giving directions with 'go straight'.",None,"straight vs direct","Straight = not bent, or directly forward. Direct = without stopping or going another way (a direct flight).","Go straightly and turn left.","Go straight and turn left."),
("ADJ-034","curly – curlier – curliest","An adjective goes directly before its noun: 'very curly hair'.","CURLY: the Y curls at the end.","'Curly brackets' { } are used in maths and coding.","Write a sentence describing someone's hair.",None,"curly vs wavy","Curly = tight rings. Wavy = loose S-shaped waves."),
("ADJ-035","flat – flatter – flattest","'Flatten' is a verb; the adjective is 'flat'.","FLAT: the word lies flat, no tall letters after F-L.","In Indian and British English, a 'flat' is also an apartment. A 'flat tyre' has no air.","Write a sentence about a flat tyre or a flat road.",None,"flat vs level","Flat = no bumps. Level = flat and horizontal, not sloping.","The tyre is flatten.","The tyre is flat."),
("ADJ-036","deep – deeper – deepest","'Depth' is the noun. After 'is', use the adjective 'deep'.","DEEP has double E, like two eyes looking down a well.","'Deep sleep', 'deep thoughts', 'deep voice' — many uses beyond water.","Write a sentence about a deep river or well you have seen.",None,"deep vs depth","Deep = adjective (a deep well). Depth = noun (the depth of the well)."),
("ADJ-037","shallow – shallower – shallowest","Before an adjective, use 'too', not 'too much'.","SHALLOW: the water is so low you can see the bottom.","A 'shallow person' cares only about surface things like looks.","Write a sentence about a shallow place where children can swim.",None,"shallow vs deep","Opposites. Shallow = little distance to the bottom. Deep = a long way down.","This pool is too much shallow.","This pool is too shallow."),
("ADJ-038","wide – wider – widest","'Width' is the noun. After 'is', use the adjective 'wide'.","WIDE: open your arms WIDE.","'Wide awake' = fully awake. 'Wide range' = many types.","Write a sentence about the widest road in your city.",None,"wide vs broad","Same meaning for distance. Broad is also used for shoulders and ideas (a broad view).","This street is very width.","This street is very wide."),
("ADJ-039","narrow – narrower – narrowest","After 'is', use the adjective 'narrow', not the adverb 'narrowly'.","NARROW: the arrow in the word is thin.","'A narrow escape' = only just escaping danger.","Write a sentence about a narrow lane in your area.",None,"narrow vs thin","Narrow = small from side to side (a road, a door). Thin = small from front to back, or not fat."),
("ADJ-040","thick – thicker – thickest","After 'was', use the adjective 'thick', not the adverb.","THICK: the CK at the end is thick and heavy.","'Thick' can describe liquids (thick soup), books, and accents.","Write a sentence about a thick book you have read.",None,"thick vs dense","Thick = wide between surfaces, or not runny. Dense = closely packed (a dense forest)."),
("ADJ-041","tight – tighter – tightest","'Feel tight' uses the adjective. 'Fit tightly' uses the adverb. Both are fine.","TIGHT: the letters sit close together.","'Money is tight' = there's not much money.","Write a sentence about clothes that became tight on you.",None,"tight vs strict","Tight = fitting closely. Strict = having firm rules (a strict teacher)."),
("ADJ-042","loose – looser – loosest","'Lose' (one O) is a verb. The adjective is 'loose' (two O's).","LOOSE has an extra O — room to move, like loose clothes.","'A loose tooth', 'loose change' (coins).","Write a sentence about something loose that needs fixing.",None,"loose vs lose","Loose (/luːs/) = not tight. Lose (/luːz/) = be unable to find, or not win.","My shirt is very lose.","My shirt is very loose."),
("ADJ-043","solid – more solid – most solid","'Proof' is uncountable, so no 'a'.","SOLID: the O's are like strong, solid balls.","'Solid' also means reliable: 'a solid plan'.","Write a sentence about a solid piece of furniture in your home.",None,"solid vs hard","Solid = not liquid or hollow, or strong. Hard = firm to touch, or difficult.","He gave me a solid proof.","He gave me solid proof."),
("ADJ-044","hollow – hollower – hollowest","'Hollow inside' is enough; 'from' is not needed.","HOLLOW: the double O is empty inside.","'Hollow promises' = promises that mean nothing.","Write a sentence about something hollow, like a pipe or a tree.",None,"hollow vs empty","Empty = has nothing in it now. Hollow = has an empty space by its design (a hollow pipe).","The tree is hollow from inside.","The tree is hollow inside."),
("ADJ-045","soft – softer – softest","Before a noun, use the adjective 'soft': 'a very soft voice'.","SOFT: the S and F sound gentle.","'Soft skills' = communication and teamwork.","Write a sentence about a soft thing you like to touch.",None,"soft vs smooth","Soft = easy to press. Smooth = even with no bumps."),
("ADJ-046","hard – harder – hardest","'Hardly' means 'almost not', not 'difficult'.","HARD vs HARDLY: adding -LY flips the meaning to 'almost not'.","'Work hard' (a lot) vs 'hardly work' (almost not). A big trap!","Write one sentence with 'hard' and one with 'hardly'.",None,"hard vs hardly","Hard = difficult, or with effort. Hardly = almost not. 'I work hard' ≠ 'I hardly work'."),
("ADJ-047","fresh – fresher – freshest","'Get fresh' isn't used for washing up. The phrase is 'freshen up'.","FRESH: the SH sounds like cool air.","Indian English 'fresher' = a new student or new employee.","Write a sentence about fresh food you buy.",None,"fresh vs new","Fresh = recently made or picked (food). New = recently made or bought (objects).","Let me get fresh first.","Let me freshen up first."),
("ADJ-048","stale – staler – stalest","'Stale' is for bread, cake and air. Milk goes 'sour' or 'off'.","STALE: rhymes with PALE — old and lifeless.","'Stale news' = old news that's no longer interesting.","Write a sentence about food that goes stale quickly.",None,"stale vs rotten","Stale = old and dry, not fresh (bread). Rotten = decayed and bad (fruit, eggs).","The milk is stale.","The milk has gone sour."),
("ADJ-049","ripe – riper – ripest","'Raw' means not cooked. Fruit that isn't ready is 'unripe'.","RIPE: rhymes with TYPE — the right type to eat.","'The time is ripe' = it's the right time.","Write a sentence about how you know a mango is ripe.",None,"ripe vs raw","Ripe = ready to eat (fruit). Raw = not cooked. Unripe = fruit not ready yet.","These bananas are raw.","These bananas are unripe."),
("ADJ-050","honest – more honest – most honest","Before a noun, use the adjective 'honest', and add 'a': 'a very honest person'.","HONEST has a silent H: say 'ON-ist', so it's 'AN honest person'.","'To be honest, …' introduces a true, sometimes blunt, opinion.","Write a sentence about an honest person you know.",None,"honest vs frank","Honest = doesn't lie or cheat. Frank = says openly what they think."),
]
